Build **Locomotivation**, a ¾-overhead (Stardew Valley-style) rail-yard hauling
arcade game that runs entirely in the browser — and **produce its art, animation,
effects, and audio yourself**, during this build, with the asset-generation tools
on your `PATH`.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. Create the game there.

## Your starting point

A starter project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`: a `package.json` that pins
**Playwright** as a dev dependency and depends on the
**`@test-cabinet/particle-runtime`** library (below). Its dependencies have already
been installed for you — `node_modules/` is populated and Playwright's headless
**Chromium** is downloaded — so you can build and verify right away. Build on this
project rather than replacing it: keep the `package.json`, add your framework, bundler,
and a `build` script on top of it, and keep the lockfile in sync (see below).

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already present
in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an implementation that
matches the specification exactly. It is decomposed by concern: `specs/overview.md`
(the pitch and fiction, goals, hard requirements, the ¾ overhead perspective, the
stage and tile grid, the palette and type, and the visual design), `specs/world.md`
(the tile grid and coordinate system, the tile kinds — ground, track, bridge, refuge,
wall, gap — corridors and forced crossings, drop zones, dispensers, spawn, and the
draw order that sells the ¾ view), `specs/character.md` (the **worker**: continuous
four-direction movement and its **four facings**, the **carry-weight speed model**,
the **recharging sprint**, carrying/multi-carry, dropping, death, and the full set of
**directional animation states**), `specs/cargo.md` (the three cargo archetypes —
**unique**, **dispenser**, **optional** — colors, weights, pickup and color-matched
delivery, and the drop / destructible-cargo rules), `specs/trains.md` (the three train
kinds, their deterministic schedules and **telegraphing**, lethal contact, junction
**switches**, and the optional derived **last-train** bonus), `specs/levels.md` (all
six campaign levels — layout, cargo, train roster, clock, and quota — each beatable),
`specs/flow.md` (the shift clock and lives, win/fail, scoring, states, menus, and HUD),
`specs/controls.md` (the fixed-timestep simulation and the keyboard controls),
`specs/assets.md` (the asset-production contract), and `specs/proof.md`.

## Build requirements

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. The **hard
requirements** — real graphics, no backend or credentials, the palette and type, a
`README.md`, and the game states — are stated in full in `specs/overview.md` (and the
files it points to). That specification is authoritative; build to it rather than to a
summary here. Three things bear restating, because the harness depends on them:

- **The fixed build/serve interface.** The project stays a **Node project** built with
  only Node.js and npm-installed dependencies. Extend the provided `package.json` at
  `{{workspace}}` and **commit a `package-lock.json`** (run `npm install` after you
  change dependencies so the lockfile matches). `npm ci` then `npm run build` must
  produce the static site — with no other manual step — into one of `dist/`, `build/`,
  or `out/` at the project root, with an `index.html` at the root of that directory.
  Serving that directory as-is must run the game, and it must work **at any base path**
  (it is played back from a per-run sub-path, so any URL the build constructs at runtime
  must be page-relative — never a leading `/`; for a bundler, set a relative base such
  as `base: './'`). `specs/overview.md` and `specs/assets.md` state this in full; follow
  it exactly.
- **You PRODUCE the art, animation, effects, and audio.** There is **no** pre-made game
  art in this project. Instead, the run image puts six asset-generation tools on your
  `PATH` — **`draw`** and **`draw-sheet`** (sprites and animated sprite sheets),
  **`particle-2d`** (particle systems), and **`sfx-synth`**, **`sfx-sample`**, and
  **`music`** (sound and music) — and you must **produce every asset the game plays with
  these tools during this build**, then wire the produced files into the game. Run each
  tool's `--help` to learn its operations. **`specs/assets.md` is the authoritative
  contract**: it enumerates every sprite, animation, particle system, and sound the game
  needs, names which tool produces each and where it lands under `assets/`, and states
  how each is wired into the build — including that the **animated worker** (the headline
  of this build: its idle/walk/sprint/carry cycles authored for each of the **four
  facings**, plus drop and squish) is a set of `draw-sheet` cycles, that the **trains**
  are produced sprites drawn as chunky ¾ side-and-top bodies, and that the **VFX** — above
  all the **required** shatter when a train destroys cargo — are `particle-2d` systems
  played live through the **`@test-cabinet/particle-runtime`** library already installed
  as a dependency of your project (import it like any other dependency; do not fetch or
  reimplement it). Load every produced file with page-relative URLs so it resolves from
  the served sub-path.
- **The reference screenshots.** If a `reference/` folder is present under `{{workspace}}`,
  its screenshots are **illustrative examples, not targets to reproduce** — design your
  own worker, trains, tiles, and layouts from the specification. Build the screens from
  the specification rather than copying any image.

## Verify before you finish

Your project has **Playwright** and a headless **Chromium** installed as project
dependencies, plus network access — use them to verify your build, do not just assume
it works. Before you consider the task complete:

- Produce the **static production build** and serve it locally, then open it in Chromium
  with Playwright and confirm it **loads with no console errors** and no uncaught
  exceptions.
- **Drive the game** — start the campaign, and on a level: pick up a color-matched
  package, watch the worker **animate** as it walks and carries and turns to face each of
  the **four directions**, cross a live track between two **telegraphed** trains and reach
  a refuge, run a **loaded** trip that costs you your sprint, **drop** a package on a track
  and watch a train **shatter** it, **deliver** to the matching zone, take a fatal hit and
  respawn with a life spent, complete the **quota** before the **shift clock** ends, and —
  on a level that offers it — board the **last train** on a flat-top car for the bonus.
  **Screenshot** the title, a live crossing, a bridge, and a result screen. Verify each
  against the specs and fix what does not follow the specification before finishing.
- **Capture the proof of implementation** exactly as `specs/proof.md` specifies: the
  screenshots and the two short clips, each written to the precise `proof/` path that
  spec names. These are required deliverables of the finished build, not a private
  check. Do not invent a different folder or filename, and do not skip the `.webm`
  clips.

Use the project's own Playwright: import it from your project (for example
`import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the project-local CLI with
`npx playwright`. There is **no** global Playwright on `PATH` — it lives in your
project's `node_modules`, like any other dependency. Launch Chromium with the
`--no-sandbox` argument — this container has no Chromium sandbox, so a default launch
will fail.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you started, with
the starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is published
directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is **not**
published. When the build is complete and verified, commit everything: run `git add -A`
then `git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working tree with nothing
uncommitted or untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes `node_modules/` and the build
output (`dist/`/`build/`/`out/`), so a clean tree does not mean committing those — but
it does mean committing your source, your `package-lock.json`, your `README.md`, the
**produced assets under `assets/`**, and the `proof/` artifacts.
